const chapters = [
{title:'A friendly hello to AI',short:'The big picture',icon:'01',desc:'Foundations & first principles'},
{title:'A little math, a lot of magic',short:'The math toolkit',icon:'02',desc:'Vectors, probability & gradients'},
{title:'Teaching machines to learn',short:'Machine learning',icon:'03',desc:'Models, data & generalization'},
{title:'How do we know it works?',short:'Evaluation & good judgment',icon:'04',desc:'Metrics, experiments & fairness'},
{title:'Inside a neural network',short:'Deep learning',icon:'05',desc:'Neurons, training & representations'},
{title:'Hello, language models',short:'LLMs & AI engineering',icon:'06',desc:'Transformers, retrieval & systems'},
{title:'Your interview toolkit',short:'Putting it all together',icon:'07',desc:'Reasoning, coding & system design'}
];
const lessons=[];
function L(ch,title,subtitle,body,rightTitle,right,example,takeaway,q,options,answer,why,extra={}){lessons.push({ch,title,subtitle,body,rightTitle,right,example,takeaway,q,options,answer,why,...extra});}
L(0,'So… what actually is AI?','Less sci-fi. More patterns.','Artificial intelligence is the study of building systems that perform tasks associated with intelligence: recognizing a voice, choosing a move, planning a route, or generating a sentence.|There is no single “AI algorithm.” Some systems follow explicit rules and search through possibilities. Others learn patterns from examples. The useful question is: what task does this system perform, and how does it decide?','Meet the family','<strong>Machine learning (ML)</strong> is a part of AI where a system learns from data. <strong>Deep learning</strong> is a part of ML that uses neural networks with multiple layers.|Generative AI produces content such as text or images. Not all AI is generative: a spam classifier simply chooses a category. These labels describe methods and capabilities, not evidence that a machine thinks like a person.','A hand-written rule that rejects messages containing one exact phrase is automation. A spam filter trained on thousands of labeled messages is machine learning. Both can be components of an AI system.','AI is the broad field. ML learns from examples. Deep learning uses layered neural networks.','Which system is an example of machine learning?',['A timer that rings after 10 minutes','A filter that learns spam patterns from labeled emails','A formula that converts Celsius to Fahrenheit'],1,'The filter learns its decision rule from examples. The timer and temperature formula follow fixed instructions.',{scene:true});
L(0,'Three ways to learn','Examples, patterns, and rewards.','In <strong>supervised learning</strong>, each training example has a target label. The model predicts the target from features. A feature is an input, such as the size of a home; the label could be its sale price.|In <strong>unsupervised learning</strong>, there are no target labels for the task. A method may group similar customers or compress a dataset. The resulting groups still need interpretation.','Learning through interaction','In <strong>reinforcement learning</strong>, an agent takes actions in an environment and receives rewards. It aims to maximize expected cumulative reward, which can require accepting a small cost now for a bigger benefit later.|Self-supervised learning creates training targets from the data itself. Predicting a hidden word is one example. It uses a supervised-style objective without people labeling every example.','A robot earns +10 for reaching a destination and −1 for each step. A short successful route earns more than a long one. Reward design matters: an agent may exploit an unintended shortcut.','Choose the learning setup from the feedback available, not from the trendiest algorithm.','You group unlabeled songs by audio similarity. What setup is this?',['Supervised learning','Unsupervised learning','Reinforcement learning'],1,'There are no known target categories or action rewards. You are discovering structure in the inputs.');
L(0,'From question to prediction','Start with the problem, not the model.','A useful ML project starts with a decision. “Predict whether a customer cancels in the next 30 days” is more actionable than “use AI on customer data.” Define who uses the prediction and what happens next.|Collect examples that represent the real setting. Define features available at prediction time, a reliable target, and a baseline. A baseline is a simple method that a complex model must beat.','Training is not inference','During <strong>training</strong>, an algorithm adjusts parameters using examples and a loss function. During <strong>inference</strong>, the trained model uses its parameters to predict for a new input.|A product also needs data validation, monitoring, and a fallback. A good offline score is not the same as a useful deployed system. Check latency, cost, and what a wrong prediction would mean.','For demand forecasting, “predict next week equals this week” is a useful baseline. If your complex model does worse on future weeks, complexity has not earned its place.','Define the decision, target, available inputs, baseline, and evaluation before choosing a model.','Which feature would leak future information into a churn prediction?',['Customer tenure today','Support tickets before prediction time','The cancellation confirmation sent next month'],2,'A future cancellation confirmation reveals the outcome. It would not exist when the prediction must be made.');
L(0,'AI beyond learning','Search, planning, and making choices.','Many AI problems can be described as a set of states and actions. A route planner starts at one location, considers roads, and searches for a goal. The solution is a sequence of actions.|Breadth-first search explores states by depth and finds a shortest path when every edge has equal cost. Uniform-cost search instead explores the cheapest accumulated path so far.','A helpful guess','A* combines the known path cost g(n) with a heuristic estimate h(n): <strong>f(n) = g(n) + h(n)</strong>. A heuristic helps prioritize promising states.|An admissible heuristic never overestimates the remaining cost. With appropriate graph-search handling, such as reopening states when needed, this supports finding an optimal path. Search still faces large state spaces and limited memory.','Straight-line distance is a useful lower bound on road travel distance. It can guide search toward a destination without pretending roads are perfectly straight.','AI includes symbolic reasoning and search, as well as statistical learning.','For A*, g(n) = 7 and h(n) = 4. What is f(n)?',null,'11','A* adds the cost already paid to the estimated remaining cost: 7 + 4 = 11.',{code:'f = 7 + 4  # result: ___'});
L(1,'Vectors are little lists','A language for describing things.','A <strong>scalar</strong> is one number. A <strong>vector</strong> is an ordered list of numbers. A matrix arranges numbers into rows and columns. An ML dataset often becomes a matrix with one row per example and one column per feature.|The order matters. If a vector is [bedrooms, area], swapping the entries changes its meaning. Shapes matter too: a matrix of 100 examples and 3 features has shape 100 × 3.','The dot product','The dot product multiplies matching entries and adds them. For x = [2, 3] and w = [4, 1], x · w = 2×4 + 3×1 = 11.|A linear prediction often takes the form <strong>ŷ = w · x + b</strong>. The weights w determine how features affect the output. The bias b shifts the prediction even when the inputs are zero.','If a tiny price model uses w = [10, 2], x = [3, 5], and b = 4, its prediction is 10×3 + 2×5 + 4 = 44. Units and feature scaling affect how weights should be interpreted.','A dot product combines features into one weighted score.','Complete the dot product: [1, 2] · [3, 4].',null,'11','Multiply matching entries and sum: 1×3 + 2×4 = 11.',{code:'dot_product = 1 * 3 + 2 * 4  # ___'});
L(1,'Probability without the panic','Uncertainty has a language.','A probability is a number between 0 and 1. It expresses uncertainty under a model or repeated-trial interpretation. A probability of 0.8 does not promise that the next event will occur.|A conditional probability, P(A | B), means the probability of A given that B is known. P(A | B) and P(B | A) are usually different. Confusing them can produce very misleading conclusions.','Base rates matter','Bayes’ rule relates them: <strong>P(A | B) = P(B | A)P(A) / P(B)</strong>, when P(B) is nonzero. The prior P(A) captures how common A was before observing B.|Suppose 1% of messages are harmful. A detector catches 90% of harmful messages and flags 10% of safe messages. In 1,000 messages, expect 9 true alarms and 99 false alarms. Only 9/108 ≈ 8.3% of alarms are true.','A detector can catch most harmful messages and still produce mostly false alarms when harmful messages are rare. Always ask about the base rate.','Conditional probabilities need context: which event is given, and how common is the event of interest?','What does P(spam | flagged) mean?',['Probability of flagging an actual spam message','Probability a flagged message is spam','Probability any message is flagged'],1,'The event after the vertical bar is the known condition. Here we know the message was flagged.');
L(1,'Learning to measure error','A loss is a score we want to shrink.','A model needs a numerical signal for how wrong its predictions are. For regression, mean squared error averages squared differences between predictions and targets: <strong>MSE = mean((ŷ − y)²)</strong>.|Squaring makes errors positive and penalizes large errors more heavily. Mean absolute error uses absolute differences and is less sensitive to extreme residuals. Neither is universally best.','Means, variance, and expectations','The mean summarizes a center. Variance measures average squared distance from that center; population variance divides by n, while a common unbiased sample estimator divides by n−1.|An expectation is a probability-weighted average. Training usually minimizes average loss over observed samples as an estimate of expected loss on the real data distribution. A biased sample can make that estimate misleading.','For targets [2, 4] and predictions [1, 6], the errors are [−1, 2]. MSE is (1 + 4)/2 = 2.5. MAE is (1 + 2)/2 = 1.5.','A loss defines what “better” means during training. Choose one that fits the task.','A model predicts 5 when the target is 2. What is its squared error?',null,'9','The residual is 5 − 2 = 3, so the squared error is 3² = 9.',{code:'squared_error = (5 - 2) ** 2  # ___'});
L(1,'A small step downhill','Gradient descent, minus the mystery.','A derivative tells us how a function changes as one input changes. A gradient collects derivatives with respect to multiple parameters. It points in the direction of steepest local increase.|To reduce a differentiable loss, move in the opposite direction: <strong>w_new = w − α∇L(w)</strong>. The learning rate α controls the step size. A large step may overshoot; a tiny step may learn slowly.','Try a training step','For the toy loss L(w) = (w − 3)², the derivative is 2(w − 3). At w = 0, the gradient is −6. With α = 0.1, the next weight is 0 − 0.1×(−6) = 0.6.|Real models use minibatches to estimate gradients. Their loss surface can be complex, and reducing training loss does not guarantee good performance on unseen data.','Try the learning-rate slider. On this particular quadratic, a learning rate above 1 makes the first step worse than the starting point. Other losses have different stable ranges.','Subtract the gradient. The learning rate controls how far you move.','Which update performs gradient descent?',['w = w + learning_rate * gradient','w = w - learning_rate * gradient','w = gradient / learning_rate'],1,'The gradient points uphill locally. Subtracting a suitably scaled gradient moves downhill.',{lab:'gradient'});
L(2,'A line that learns','Your first regression model.','Linear regression predicts a numerical value as a weighted sum of inputs plus a bias. With one feature, it draws a line: <strong>ŷ = wx + b</strong>. “Linear” refers to the parameters; engineered features can include x² or other transformations.|Training chooses weights that reduce an objective, often squared error. A coefficient represents a conditional association within the chosen model, not proof of causation.','Read the line','The slope w tells you how much the prediction changes when x increases by one, holding the other inputs constant. The intercept b is the prediction at x = 0, which may be outside the meaningful data range.|Outliers, correlated features, and extrapolation can make a fitted model fragile. Plot residuals and compare against a baseline instead of relying on a single score.','A model predicts study score = 5×hours + 40. At 4 hours, it predicts 60. At 20 hours, it predicts 140: a reminder that a line need not respect real-world limits.','A simple model is useful when its assumptions and limits are understood.','With w = 3, x = 4, and b = 2, what is the prediction?',null,'14','ŷ = wx + b = 3×4 + 2 = 14.',{code:'prediction = 3 * 4 + 2  # ___'});
L(2,'From scores to categories','Logistic regression is a classifier.','For binary classification, logistic regression takes a linear score z = w · x + b and applies the sigmoid: <strong>p = 1 / (1 + exp(−z))</strong>. This maps any finite score to a value between 0 and 1.|Training commonly uses binary cross-entropy. It penalizes confident wrong predictions strongly. Despite its name, logistic regression is usually used for classification.','A probability is not a decision','To turn a probability into a label, choose a threshold. Predict positive when p ≥ threshold. Lowering the threshold generally increases the number of positive predictions, trading more true positives for more false positives.|A threshold of 0.5 is a convention, not a law. Choose a threshold using validation data and the costs of different errors. Check calibration if the probability values themselves drive decisions.','For an alerting system, a missed critical event and an unnecessary alert may have very different costs. The right threshold depends on both costs and the event rate.','Training a score and choosing an action threshold are separate decisions.','If p = 0.65 and the threshold is 0.7, which label is predicted?',['Positive','Negative','The model cannot decide'],1,'0.65 is below the chosen 0.7 threshold, so this decision rule predicts negative.',{lab:'threshold'});
L(2,'Trees, neighbors, and teams','Different models bring different biases.','A decision tree repeatedly splits the data using feature tests. Trees can capture nonlinear relationships without requiring you to write interactions by hand, but deep trees can memorize training data.|A random forest averages many trees trained with randomness. Averaging reduces variance when their errors are not perfectly correlated. Gradient boosting instead adds models sequentially to improve an objective.','No universal winner','k-nearest neighbors predicts using nearby examples. It needs a useful distance metric, and scaling can strongly affect which points count as neighbors. Prediction can be expensive on large datasets.|k-means is different: it groups unlabeled points around k centroids to reduce within-cluster squared distance. Initialization, feature scaling, and the chosen k affect results; clusters are not automatically meaningful categories.','With features “age” and “income,” raw income differences may dominate distance. Standardizing using training-set statistics can make both features contribute more comparably.','Pick models based on data, error costs, latency, and validation—not a universal leaderboard.','Which method builds many randomized trees and averages their predictions?',['Random forest','k-means','A single deep decision tree'],0,'A random forest ensembles trees. k-means clusters points, and one tree has no ensemble averaging.');
L(2,'Memorizing is not learning','The real goal is generalization.','Underfitting happens when a model cannot capture useful patterns. Both training and validation performance may be poor. Overfitting happens when a model fits quirks of the training sample that do not transfer.|A large training-validation gap suggests high variance or a distribution mismatch. More representative data, a simpler model, regularization, or early stopping can help, but investigate the cause first.','Keep a fair final exam','Use training data to fit parameters, validation data to select settings, and a held-out test set for final evaluation. Repeatedly selecting models based on the test score turns it into another validation set.|Fit preprocessing only on training data. Use group-aware splits when the same person appears repeatedly, and time-based splits when predicting the future. Random splits are not always appropriate.','If photos of the same patient appear in both training and testing, the model may recognize the patient rather than generalize to new patients. Split by patient to match the intended use.','The split must reflect what “new data” will mean after deployment.','Where should you fit a feature scaler before evaluating a held-out test set?',['On the full dataset','On the test set','On the training set only'],2,'Learn preprocessing statistics from training data, then apply the same transformation to held-out data. This prevents information leakage.');
L(3,'Accuracy can be a trap','Count the kinds of mistakes.','For a binary classifier, a true positive is a positive case correctly flagged. A false positive is a negative case incorrectly flagged. A false negative is a positive case missed. A true negative is a negative case correctly ignored.|Accuracy is the fraction of all predictions that are correct. If only 1% of examples are positive, always predicting negative yields 99% accuracy while detecting no positives.','Precision and recall','<strong>Precision = TP / (TP + FP)</strong>: among the predicted positives, how many were right? <strong>Recall = TP / (TP + FN)</strong>: among the actual positives, how many were found?|F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall: 2PR/(P+R). It ignores true negatives and does not encode every business cost. Always inspect sample counts and define handling for zero denominators.','A detector finds 8 true positives, produces 2 false positives, and misses 12 positives. Precision is 8/10 = 80%; recall is 8/20 = 40%. Good precision can coexist with low recall.','Choose metrics that reveal the mistakes your application actually cares about.','With TP = 6 and FP = 4, what is precision?',['60%','40%','We need the true negatives'],0,'Precision is 6/(6+4) = 0.6. True negatives do not appear in this formula.');
L(3,'A score needs context','Thresholds, rankings, and confidence.','A confusion matrix describes performance at one threshold. ROC curves vary the threshold to plot true-positive rate against false-positive rate. ROC-AUC summarizes ranking performance across thresholds.|With rare positives, precision-recall curves can make the practical tradeoffs clearer. The baseline precision depends on prevalence, so comparisons across populations need care.','Calibrated confidence','A calibrated model that assigns probability 0.8 should be correct about 80% of the time among comparable predictions assigned that probability. Good ranking does not guarantee good calibration.|A score estimated from a small test sample is uncertain. Report sample size and uncertainty, and inspect slices such as language, region, or device. An average can hide a serious failure in a smaller group.','Two models can rank examples in the same order while producing probabilities like [0.51, 0.52] versus [0.1, 0.9]. Their ranking may match, but their confidence claims differ.','Ask whether you need good ranking, calibrated probabilities, or a good decision at a particular threshold.','A well-calibrated model assigns 0.7 to 100 comparable cases. Roughly how many positives should you expect?',null,'70','Calibration means the long-run positive rate matches the probability: 0.7×100 = 70. A finite sample need not contain exactly 70.',{code:'expected_positives = 0.7 * 100  # ___'});
L(3,'Make experiments trustworthy','Better evidence, fewer lucky wins.','Cross-validation trains and evaluates across multiple folds to estimate how a modeling procedure generalizes. Preprocessing must be fit separately inside each training fold. Keep dependent examples together.|Hyperparameters, such as tree depth, are choices outside the fitted model parameters. Tune them using validation results. Trying hundreds of settings can overfit the validation set too.','From offline to online','An offline metric estimates performance on a dataset. An A/B experiment randomly assigns eligible users or units to variants to estimate the effect of a product change. They answer different questions.|Define the primary metric, guardrails, assignment unit, and duration in advance. Check sample-size needs and delayed outcomes. Repeatedly checking results and stopping at the first “win” can inflate false discoveries.','A recommender may improve offline ranking but reduce long-term satisfaction if it only shows repetitive content. An online experiment needs a user-centered outcome and guardrails.','A valid comparison requires consistent data, appropriate splits, and a decision rule chosen before seeing results.','Should you fit preprocessing once on all data before cross-validation?',['Yes, because preprocessing is not learning','No, fit it within each training fold','Only if the dataset is small'],1,'Preprocessing learns statistics from data. Fitting on all folds leaks information from each validation fold.');
L(3,'Useful, fair, and responsible','People live on the other side of a prediction.','A model can reproduce biases in its training data, labels, or measurement process. Removing a sensitive feature does not eliminate proxy information. Other variables may encode related signals.|Measure relevant outcomes across groups, inspect the consequences of different errors, and involve people affected by the system. Fairness criteria can conflict, so a single number cannot settle every tradeoff.','Build for failure','Collect only necessary data and control access. Document the intended use, limits, and evaluation. For important decisions, design meaningful human review rather than a button that rubber-stamps predictions.|Monitor changes in input distributions and outcome performance. Drift is a signal to investigate, not automatic proof that retraining will help. Provide a fallback and a way to report mistakes.','An automated screening tool may perform well overall while missing a smaller language group. Aggregate accuracy hides that harm; slice evaluation and a workable appeal process can reveal and address it.','Responsible AI is part of data collection, evaluation, deployment, and monitoring—not a final checkbox.','Which action is most useful when overall accuracy hides poor subgroup recall?',['Report only the overall number','Delete every sensitive feature and assume the issue is solved','Investigate subgroup data and errors, then evaluate mitigations'],2,'Investigate the causes and consequences. Removing a feature alone cannot ensure fair outcomes, especially when proxies remain.');
L(4,'Meet a tiny neuron','Weighted sums, with a twist.','A neural-network unit takes inputs, computes a weighted sum plus a bias, and applies an activation function: <strong>a = activation(w · x + b)</strong>. A layer contains multiple such units.|ReLU returns max(0, z). Sigmoid maps a value into (0, 1). Nonlinear activations allow a network to represent patterns that a chain of purely linear layers cannot.','Layers learn representations','Early layers transform the input into intermediate features. Later layers combine them into representations useful for the objective. These features are learned; they need not match human concepts neatly.|The output layer depends on the task: a regression model can output a number, while a multiclass classifier commonly uses softmax to produce class scores that sum to one.','Without nonlinearities, composing linear layers still produces a linear mapping. Adding more linear layers alone does not create the expressive power usually associated with deep networks.','A neuron is a computation, not a miniature human brain cell.','What is ReLU(−3)?',null,'0','ReLU(z) = max(0, z), so negative inputs become zero.',{code:'activation = max(0, -3)  # ___'});
L(4,'How a network learns','Forward, backward, update.','The forward pass computes predictions and loss. Backpropagation uses the chain rule to compute how each parameter affects that loss. An optimizer then updates parameters using those gradients.|Backpropagation and gradient descent are not synonyms: one computes gradients; the other uses them to change parameters. Automatic differentiation makes this practical for complex computation graphs.','A training loop','A minibatch is a subset of training examples used for one update. An epoch is one full pass through the training dataset, subject to how the training loop samples and batches examples.|In a typical PyTorch loop: clear accumulated gradients, run the forward pass, compute loss, call loss.backward(), then optimizer.step(). Validate separately, without using validation examples to update parameters.','If gradients are not cleared between independent updates in a framework that accumulates them, the next update includes earlier gradients. Deliberate gradient accumulation is useful, but accidental accumulation changes training.','Backprop computes gradients. The optimizer changes weights. Validation checks whether learning transfers.','Complete the PyTorch call that computes gradients from a scalar loss.',null,'backward','loss.backward() computes gradients. optimizer.step() applies an update using them.',{code:'loss.___()'});
L(4,'Help your network generalize','Good habits beat bigger models.','L2 regularization adds a penalty related to squared parameter magnitude. It discourages large weights and can reduce overfitting. Weight decay is related, but its relationship to L2 depends on the optimizer.|Dropout randomly removes some activations during training. At inference, standard dropout is disabled with the framework’s appropriate scaling behavior. It encourages less dependence on particular activations.','Watch the learning curves','If training loss keeps falling while validation loss rises, consider early stopping, regularization, data quality, and model capacity. Save the best validation checkpoint rather than assuming the last epoch is best.|Use training and evaluation modes correctly. Layers such as dropout and batch normalization behave differently in these modes. Turning off gradient tracking is separate from selecting evaluation mode.','For an evaluation pass in PyTorch, model.eval() changes mode-sensitive layers; a no-gradient context avoids recording gradients. One does not replace the other.','Model capacity, data quality, optimization, and regularization work together.','Training loss falls while validation loss rises. What is a plausible diagnosis?',['The model may be overfitting','The model has definitely learned the true causal process','The validation set should be added to training immediately'],0,'This divergence suggests overfitting, though split problems or distribution shift should also be investigated.');
L(4,'Networks with different talents','Images, sequences, and similarity.','Convolutional neural networks reuse filters across spatial locations. This weight sharing makes them useful for images and other signals with local structure. Pooling or strides can reduce spatial resolution.|Recurrent networks process sequences using a hidden state. They reuse parameters over time, but long-range dependencies can be difficult to learn. Gated variants such as LSTMs help manage information flow.','Embeddings put meaning in numbers','An embedding maps an item, word, or image into a vector. Training encourages useful relationships in that vector space. “Useful” depends on the objective and data, not an inherent universal meaning.|Cosine similarity compares vector directions: (a · b)/(‖a‖‖b‖), for nonzero vectors. Similar embeddings can support search, but similarity does not prove equivalence or factual correctness.','A search system embeds a question and candidate documents, then retrieves nearby vectors. It may find related text that does not actually answer the question, so relevance still needs evaluation.','Architecture adds assumptions about the structure of data. Representation quality depends on the learning objective.','Which architecture uses shared filters across image locations?',['A convolutional neural network','A lookup table with no learning','A single scalar threshold'],0,'Convolutions reuse the same filter weights across locations, exploiting local spatial structure.');
L(5,'Words become tokens','The model sees numbers first.','A tokenizer converts text into discrete token IDs. A token may be a word, part of a word, punctuation, or bytes, depending on the tokenizer. Token count is not the same as word count.|A language model learns a probability distribution over sequences. In autoregressive training, it predicts the next token from earlier tokens; generating a response repeats that prediction process.','Fluent is not necessarily factual','Training rewards predicting plausible tokens under the objective. It does not directly verify every statement against the world. A model can produce confident, grammatical text that is wrong.|The context window is the amount of token context a model can process in a given interaction. The exact limit varies. Providing a fact in context is not the same as permanently updating model weights.','The phrase “unbelievable!” might become several tokens. Different tokenizers can split it differently, so use the actual tokenizer when estimating input length.','Tokens are the model’s discrete inputs. A plausible continuation is not a fact-check.','Does adding a paragraph to a prompt normally update the model’s weights?',['Yes, permanently','No, it provides context for this inference','Only when the paragraph contains numbers'],1,'Ordinary inference conditions on the prompt. Parameter updates require a training or adaptation process.');
L(5,'Attention, please','Let relevant tokens talk to each other.','Self-attention lets each position combine information from other permitted positions. It computes query, key, and value vectors from the representations. Query-key compatibility determines how much weight each value receives.|Scaled dot-product attention is <strong>softmax(QKᵀ / √dₖ)V</strong>. The scale helps control score magnitude. Multiple heads learn different projections and can capture different relationships.','Order and causality matter','A transformer also uses feed-forward layers, residual connections, normalization, and position information. Attention by itself does not inherently encode token order.|In a causal decoder, a mask prevents a position from attending to future tokens during training. Standard full attention has quadratic attention-score memory and computation in sequence length; implementation details and variants can change practical costs.','In “The cat slept because it was tired,” attention can help connect “it” to relevant earlier context. Attention weights are not automatically a faithful explanation of the whole model’s reasoning.','Queries ask, keys match, values contribute. A causal mask keeps next-token training honest.','Why mask future tokens during causal language-model training?',['To prevent seeing the answer being predicted','To remove all nouns','To make token order irrelevant'],0,'A next-token predictor must not use future context that will be unavailable during generation.');
L(5,'Give your model an open book','Retrieval-augmented generation.','RAG retrieves relevant external material and supplies it as context before generation. A typical pipeline splits documents into chunks, indexes them, retrieves candidates for a query, and asks the model to answer from that evidence.|Embedding retrieval finds semantic matches. Keyword retrieval can help with exact names and identifiers. Hybrid retrieval and reranking can improve candidate selection, but add complexity and latency.','Measure the whole pipeline','Evaluate retrieval separately from generation. Did the needed source appear in the retrieved set? Did the answer accurately use it? Were the citations faithful? A polished answer can hide a retrieval failure.|Respect document permissions during retrieval. Treat retrieved text as untrusted input: it may contain malicious instructions. Provide an “insufficient evidence” path when the sources do not support an answer.','For a company handbook assistant, retrieve the current leave policy, provide the relevant passages, and ask for a cited answer. If the policy is missing, abstaining is better than inventing a benefit.','RAG supplies evidence at inference time. It can reduce unsupported answers, but cannot guarantee correctness.','A correct source never appears in the retrieved context. What should you investigate first?',['Only the font size of the response','Retrieval, indexing, chunking, and query matching','Whether the answer sounds confident'],1,'The generation stage cannot reliably use evidence it never received. Debug retrieval before changing the answer style.');
L(5,'Turn a model into a product','Reliability lives around the model.','Prompting specifies the task and context. Fine-tuning updates weights using training examples and can improve behavior or format consistency. RAG supplies external evidence. These techniques solve different problems and can be combined.|Start with representative evaluation cases and a simple baseline. Measure task success, groundedness where relevant, latency, and cost. Include adversarial, ambiguous, and out-of-scope requests.','Tools need boundaries','A tool-using agent chooses actions such as searches or API calls. Validate arguments and outputs, scope permissions, and require appropriate confirmation for consequential actions. Model-generated text is not authorization.|Use timeouts, bounded retries, logging that avoids unnecessary sensitive data, and graceful fallbacks. Cache carefully: keys must account for permissions and relevant context so one user does not receive another user’s data.','For a support assistant, track grounded answer quality and escalation quality alongside response time. A shorter answer that fails the task is not a successful cost optimization.','Evaluate the complete system, including retrieval, permissions, tools, and failure handling.','You need answers based on frequently changing private documents. What is a sensible starting point?',['RAG with permission-aware retrieval','Assume pretrained weights contain every new document','Fine-tune once and stop updating anything'],0,'Retrieval can supply current authorized evidence without retraining for every document update. Evaluate its coverage and answer grounding.');
L(6,'Explain it like an engineer','Clear reasoning is part of the skill.','A strong interview answer defines the concept, gives an example, and explains a tradeoff. If the question is ambiguous, state assumptions. You do not need to hide uncertainty behind jargon.|For “bias versus variance,” explain that a restrictive model can miss patterns, while a highly flexible model can be sensitive to the training sample. Connect that idea to observed learning curves and possible interventions.','Use a practical answer structure','Try: <strong>goal → data → baseline → model → evaluation → failure modes</strong>. When discussing a method, explain why it fits the problem and what evidence would make you change your mind.|For a failure investigation, propose a check before a fix. “I would inspect temporal leakage and train-serving differences” is more informative than “I would use a larger model.”','Question: Why not use accuracy for rare-event detection? Answer: a majority-class predictor can score highly while missing every event. I would report precision-recall tradeoffs at thresholds matched to error costs.','Good answers connect definitions to decisions, assumptions, and evidence.','A model has excellent test scores but fails in production. What is a strong first step?',['Immediately double the layer count','Check leakage, distribution shift, and train-serving consistency','Assume users are wrong'],1,'Verify the data and evaluation assumptions before selecting a more complex model.');
L(6,'A tiny coding interview','Write the simple version clearly.','Interview coding often tests whether you can manipulate data, implement a metric, and handle edge cases. State input shapes and expected output before coding. Walk through a tiny example by hand.|For a mean, sum the values and divide by their count. An empty list needs an explicit policy: raise an error, return a sentinel, or follow the application contract. Silently dividing by zero is not a policy.','Reason about complexity','A loop over n values takes O(n) time. Computing a running sum and count uses O(1) extra space. Sorting first would add work that calculating a mean does not require.|For ML code, also ask about numerical stability, missing values, data types, and leakage. A correct formula can still fail on realistic inputs. Explain which edge cases you are handling.','For a stable softmax, subtract the largest logit before exponentiating: exp(zᵢ − max(z)) / Σ exp(zⱼ − max(z)). The probabilities are unchanged mathematically, but overflow risk is reduced.','Correctness includes the input contract, edge cases, and computational cost.','Complete the denominator for a mean of a nonempty Python list named values.',null,'len(values)','The arithmetic mean is sum(values) divided by the number of elements, len(values).',{code:'mean = sum(values) / ___'});
L(6,'Design a system out loud','There is more than one reasonable architecture.','Suppose you are asked to design a product recommender. Clarify users, catalog size, traffic, latency budget, available feedback, and the objective. Clicks, purchases, and long-term satisfaction are not interchangeable labels.|A common design separates candidate retrieval from ranking. Retrieval narrows a large catalog; a richer ranker scores a smaller candidate set. Filters enforce availability, permissions, and other constraints.','Walk through the lifecycle','Explain offline training, time-aware evaluation, online serving, logging, and monitoring. Handle cold starts for new users and items. A popularity or content-based fallback can work before enough interaction data arrives.|Discuss feedback loops: the system mostly observes reactions to items it chose to show. Position and exposure bias can distort labels. Controlled exploration and experiments help, subject to product constraints.','If a ranker meets quality targets but takes two seconds per request, it may be unusable. Reduce candidates, simplify features, batch work, or cache appropriately—and remeasure quality after each change.','System design is a sequence of explicit tradeoffs under real constraints.','Why split recommendation into retrieval and ranking?',['To avoid evaluation','To make expensive scoring feasible on a small candidate set','To guarantee every recommendation is relevant'],1,'Candidate retrieval reduces the search space so a more expensive ranker can operate within serving constraints.');
L(6,'Your next chapter starts here','Turn reading into evidence of skill.','You now have a map of the field. Build fluency by retrieving ideas from memory, solving problems, and implementing projects. Reading these lessons alone cannot guarantee a course grade or an interview outcome.|Start with a tabular prediction project: define the target, prevent leakage, fit a baseline and a model, compare suitable metrics, and write an error analysis. Keep the final test set untouched until your selection is complete.','Build, explain, reflect','Then build a small document assistant with permission-aware retrieval, cited answers, and a fixed evaluation set. Measure retrieval failures separately from unsupported answers. Document latency, cost, and what happens when evidence is missing.|For a broader AI course, extend into constraint satisfaction, adversarial search, Bayesian networks, and reinforcement-learning algorithms. Match your practice to the actual course syllabus and target interview role.','A useful project report says what you tried, why the split matches the intended deployment, what the model beats, where it fails, and what you would investigate next. Honest limitations show judgment.','Use this book as a foundation and review companion. Practice coding, mathematics, and explaining tradeoffs without looking at the page.','What is the strongest evidence that you can apply these concepts?',['Memorizing model names','Building and evaluating a project, then explaining its limitations','Completing one quiz with the book open'],1,'Applied work exposes choices and failure modes that recognition-based questions cannot. Pair projects with closed-book recall and coding practice.');
const interviewPrompts = [
['Your classifier gets 99% accuracy. Is it good?','Ask about class balance, the baseline, the confusion matrix, error costs, data splits, and performance on relevant slices. Accuracy alone is insufficient.'],
['Training performance is strong; validation performance is weak. What next?','Investigate overfitting, leakage in training, split representativeness, and distribution shift. Inspect learning curves; consider more representative data, regularization, simpler models, and early stopping.'],
['Explain gradient descent to a beginner.','It repeatedly measures a local uphill direction of the loss, then takes a controlled step in the opposite direction. Explain the learning rate, a simple numerical example, and why training loss is not the final goal.'],
['When would you choose RAG over fine-tuning?','Use retrieval when answers need current, attributable external information. Fine-tuning changes behavior or task performance through weight updates. They can be combined; compare with a representative evaluation set.'],
['Design an assistant for private company documents.','Clarify users and permissions. Discuss ingestion, updates, chunking, retrieval, reranking, evidence-based generation, citations, permission enforcement, prompt injection, abstention, evaluation, latency, and monitoring.'],
['How would you investigate production performance degradation?','Check data quality, feature availability and transformations, drift, label delays, model and data versions, and serving errors. Segment the issue, compare against a baseline, mitigate safely, and verify the cause before retraining.'],
['Implement precision and recall. What are the edge cases?','Define positive class and count TP, FP, and FN. Precision = TP/(TP+FP); recall = TP/(TP+FN). Define zero-denominator behavior explicitly, check shapes, and test all-negative and all-positive predictions.'],
['How would you design a recommender for a new app?','Clarify the objective and constraints. Begin with a popularity or content baseline, address cold starts, then consider retrieval and ranking. Use time-aware offline evaluation, online experiments, and monitoring for feedback loops.']
];
